---
export const prerender = true;

import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import {
  OutcomeBalance,
  CorrChart,
  AccuracyChart,
} from "../components/AnalysisCharts.tsx";
import stats from "../data/dataset-stats.json";
import metrics from "../data/model-metrics.json";

const features = Object.entries(stats.features).map(([name, f]) => ({
  name,
  overall: f.overall,
  diabetic: f.diabetic,
  healthy: f.healthy,
  corr: f.correlation_with_outcome,
}));

const corrRows = [...features]
  .sort((a, b) => b.corr - a.corr)
  .map((f) => ({ feature: f.name, corr: f.corr }));

const accRows = metrics.models.map((m) => ({
  model: m.name,
  accuracy: m.accuracy,
}));
---

<Layout title="Analysis — Diabetes Capstone">
  <div class="px-8 py-8 space-y-10">
    <header>
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-wide text-blue-600 uppercase">
        Unit 2 · Data Science Methodology (Rollins / IBM) · SDG 3
      </p>
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-1">
        Capstone Analysis, Step by Step
      </h1>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2">
        Every number below is computed from our dataset
        (<code>{stats.source}</code>: {stats.rows} rows, {stats.diabetic}{" "}
        diabetic / {stats.healthy} healthy) — run <code>bun run data</code> to
        regenerate.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-gray-100">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">
        1 · Business Understanding — from problem to approach
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">
        <strong>Problem:</strong> diabetes is often detected late, after
        complications set in. <strong>Users:</strong> individuals and families
        who want an early, understandable risk signal from routine health
        numbers. <strong>Goal:</strong> a predictive classifier (will this
        profile look diabetic?) plus prescriptive coaching (what to do next),
        aligned with <strong>SDG 3: Good Health &amp; Well-being</strong>.
        Analytic approach: binary classification on 8 numeric health features.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-gray-100">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">
        2 · Data Requirements — 5W1H
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">
        <strong>What:</strong> 8 inputs (Pregnancies, Glucose, BloodPressure,
        SkinThickness, Insulin, BMI, DiabetesPedigreeFunction, Age) + Outcome
        label. <strong>Where:</strong> structured CSV table.{" "}
        <strong>How much:</strong> {stats.rows} labelled rows ({Math.round(
          stats.diabetic_rate * 100,
        )}% positive) — enough for classical ML, too small for deep learning.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-gray-100">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">3 · Data Collection</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 mb-4">
        Secondary data: the Healthcare-Diabetes CSV (Pima-style). Credible,
        permission-free for education. Ethics: de-identified rows, no names;
        our app stores personal check-ins only in the visitor's own browser.
        Known bias: zeros in Glucose/BloodPressure/SkinThickness/Insulin/BMI
        are missing-value placeholders, not real measurements.
      </p>
      <OutcomeBalance
        healthy={stats.healthy}
        diabetic={stats.diabetic}
        client:load
      />
    </section>

    <section class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-gray-100">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">4 · Data Understanding</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 mb-4">
        Glucose correlates strongest with the outcome ({stats.features.Glucose
          .correlation_with_outcome}); diabetic medians sit clearly above
        healthy medians on Glucose ({stats.features.Glucose.diabetic.median} vs{" "}
        {stats.features.Glucose.healthy.median}), BMI (
        {stats.features.BMI.diabetic.median} vs{" "}
        {stats.features.BMI.healthy.median}) and Age (
        {stats.features.Age.diabetic.median} vs{" "}
        {stats.features.Age.healthy.median}).
      </p>
      <CorrChart rows={corrRows} client:load />
      <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-6">
        <table class="w-full text-xs">
          <thead>
            <tr class="text-left text-gray-500 border-b">
              <th class="py-2 pr-3">Feature</th>
              <th class="py-2 pr-3">Mean</th>
              <th class="py-2 pr-3">Median</th>
              <th class="py-2 pr-3">Diabetic median</th>
              <th class="py-2 pr-3">Healthy median</th>
              <th class="py-2 pr-3">Corr. w/ outcome</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              features.map((f) => (
                <tr class="border-b last:border-0">
                  <td class="py-2 pr-3 font-medium">{f.name}</td>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-3">{f.overall.mean}</td>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-3">{f.overall.median}</td>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-3">{f.diabetic.median}</td>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-3">{f.healthy.median}</td>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-3">{f.corr}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-gray-100">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">5 · Data Preparation</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">
        Dropped the <code>Id</code> column (identifier, not signal). Clipped
        outliers to the 50th–95th percentile band during training exploration.
        Split {metrics.split.train} train / {metrics.split.test} test
        (test_size={metrics.split.test_size}, random_state={
          metrics.split.random_state
        }). No scaling — tree models don't need it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-gray-100">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">6 · AI Modelling</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 mb-4">
        Trained SVM (linear), Decision Tree and Random Forest (
        {metrics.models[2].params}). Deployed:{" "}
        <strong>{metrics.deployed}</strong> — {metrics.deploy_reason}
      </p>
      <AccuracyChart rows={accRows} client:load />
    </section>

    <section class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-gray-100">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-4">7 · Evaluation</h2>
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
        {
          metrics.models
            .filter((m) => m.cm)
            .map((m) => (
              <div>
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-sm mb-2">
                  {m.name} — accuracy {m.accuracy}
                </h3>
                <table class="text-xs border">
                  <thead>
                    <tr class="bg-gray-50">
                      <th class="px-3 py-1" />
                      <th class="px-3 py-1">Pred 0</th>
                      <th class="px-3 py-1">Pred 1</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr class="border-t">
                      <td class="px-3 py-1 font-medium">True 0</td>
                      <td class="px-3 py-1">{m.cm[0][0]}</td>
                      <td class="px-3 py-1">{m.cm[0][1]}</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr class="border-t">
                      <td class="px-3 py-1 font-medium">True 1</td>
                      <td class="px-3 py-1">{m.cm[1][0]}</td>
                      <td class="px-3 py-1">{m.cm[1][1]}</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
                <ul class="text-xs text-gray-600 mt-2 space-y-1">
                  <li>
                    Class 0: P {m.report["0"].precision} · R{" "}
                    {m.report["0"].recall} · F1 {m.report["0"].f1}
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    Class 1: P {m.report["1"].precision} · R{" "}
                    {m.report["1"].recall} · F1 {m.report["1"].f1}
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            ))
        }
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 mt-4">
        Read: the tree memorises the test split (96%) — classic overfit on
        noisy tabular data — while SVM underfits the minority class (recall
        0.46 on diabetics). The forest balances both, and its probability
        output doubles as our risk score.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-gray-100">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">8 · Deployment</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">
        Static Astro frontend on a Cloudflare Worker (this site) → prediction
        API on Render (FastAPI + <code>model.pkl</code>) → SHAP-style
        explanation bars → Gemini health coach. One repo, free tiers, no
        servers to babysit.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-gray-100">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">9–10 · Feedback loop</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">
        The <a href="/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">My Tracking</a>{" "}
        tab closes the loop: routine per-person check-ins are scored against
        the confirmed-diabetic zone (bad-region bands + centroid distance +
        risk slope). Drift toward the bad zone triggers early-sign flags —
        deployment feedback that a static one-shot predictor can never give.
        Next iteration: retrain on volunteered (anonymised) check-ins.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
